voice:
- Direct and specific. No filler enthusiasm and never use "I am excited to apply."
- Open with a concrete fit, problem, method, or piece of experience. Do not use generic
  application language.
- Do not use em dashes. Prefer commas, colons, semicolons, or separate sentences.
- Three or four body paragraphs, usually 350-450 words total unless the posting calls
  for something shorter.
- At least one paragraph should bridge a real past task to a specific duty in the posting.
- Never claim experience with a tool, system, method, or domain not supported by the
  facts above.
- Do not turn a cover letter into a self-assessment. Mention at most one material gap,
  and only when hiding it would be misleading.
- No superlatives about the employer and no generic flattery.
- Prefer concrete nouns, verbs, methods, datasets, and outcomes over abstract claims
  about passion or fit.
- Treat the Automated Job Intelligence System as private evidence of capability, not
  as a default resume or cover-letter project.
- Do not name, describe, or feature the Automated Job Intelligence System in application
  materials unless the role specifically centers on agentic AI, LLM systems, prompt
  engineering, AI workflow orchestration, or closely related tooling.
- Skills demonstrated by the Automated Job Intelligence System, such as agentic workflow
  design, prompt engineering, API integration, structured-output design, automation,
  and persistent workflow state, may be listed or described when directly relevant
  without volunteering that the underlying project automated a personal job search.
- For ordinary quantitative, data, economics, policy, research, consulting, or finance
  roles, omit the Automated Job Intelligence System entirely and prioritize domain-relevant
  projects and supervised work.
- Never invent a different project or employer context to explain skills learned through
  the Automated Job Intelligence System. If detailed evidence is requested, describe
  the underlying work accurately or omit the claim.
- Distinguish direct model execution from research support. For causal inference at
  the School of Criminal Justice, state that the candidate collected and prepared data,
  researched modeling choices, and discussed specifications; do not claim direct participation
  in final model estimation.
- Write like a technically literate person talking to another person, not like an academic
  abstract. Do not repeatedly use labels such as "longitudinal," "cross-sectional,"
  "quantitative research," "stakeholder," "rigorous," "methodological," or "research
  synthesis" when plain English communicates the same fact. Prefer "tracked over four
  years" to "longitudinal," "worked with" to "stakeholder engagement," and concrete
  descriptions of what was done over category labels. Technical terminology should
  appear when it conveys information, not merely because it exists in the fact base.
- Avoid repeating a distinctive technical or professional term more than once in a
  document unless repetition is necessary for accuracy or a job-specific keyword.
- Bullets should be broken into discrete ideas. Do not write list-like sentences such
  as "did X, Y, Z, A, B, C."
- Avoid the contrastive X-not-Y tic. Across a document, do not use constructions built
  around "rather than," "instead of," "not X but Y," or a similar manufactured contrast;
  unless not doing so would explicitly lead to awkward phrasing. State the action directly
  whenever the contrast is not essential.
- Resume bullets should state what was done, how it was done when relevant, and what
  came out of it. Do not append a methodological defense, a mini-lesson, or an explanation
  of why the choice was smarter than an alternative.
- Do not turn a resume bullet into commentary for a grader. Phrases such as "to ensure
  rigor," "to demonstrate discipline," "so the result was trustworthy," or "the same
  discipline required by..." should usually be cut unless they describe an actual measured
  outcome.
- A cover letter must add information or perspective that the resume cannot hold. Do
  not translate a resume bullet into prose and then repeat its methods and metrics.
  When discussing a project already on the resume, use the letter for a decision, surprise,
  failed approach, tradeoff, debugging lesson, changed assumption, or reason the problem
  mattered.
- Do not force every cover-letter paragraph into the same structure of abstract topic
  sentence, evidence, and concluding bridge. Vary paragraph length and entry point.
  Let one paragraph begin with a concrete event or decision if that is more natural.
- Avoid stock AI transitions and self-summaries such as "that discipline carries," "maps
  directly onto," "the bridge from," "what I bring," "the clearest starting point,"
  "that experience taught me," "this aligns directly," and "the core of what I have
  done." Use plain transitions or none.
- Use normal technical punctuation. Ordinary hyphens belong in compound modifiers such
  as "walk-forward," "point-in-time," "state-space," "cross-sectional," and "non-technical."
  Use an en dash for numeric or date ranges when appropriate and the multiplication
  sign in forms such as "400x400." Do not strip normal technical punctuation to satisfy
  the no-em-dash rule.
- In generated source text, use ordinary visible hyphens, not the Unicode nonbreaking
  hyphen character. Document formatting may prevent awkward line breaks without changing
  the visible punctuation.
- Do not upgrade a method, project, or context merely because a posting uses a stronger
  keyword. Descriptive estimation is not automatically predictive modeling; temporal
  validation is not automatically forecasting; statistical research is not automatically
  machine learning; numerical computation is not automatically numerical optimization;
  transaction data is not automatically financial-market microstructure.
- Do not call code "production-grade" unless the facts establish deployment, reliability,
  monitoring, operational ownership, or another concrete production criterion. "Built,"
  "implemented," or "automated" is usually enough.
- Do not turn research support into direct model execution. Preserve the distinction
  between preparing data, researching methods, discussing specifications, and actually
  estimating a final model.
- Prefer human-scale explanations over category labels. If a sentence can say "tracked
  1,052 staffers over four years," do not default to "conducted longitudinal analysis."
  If it can say "worked with partner organizations," do not default to "stakeholder
  engagement."
- Do not repeat a project's full stack or method list in both the resume and cover letter.
  The resume can carry the inventory; the letter should select only the one or two
  details needed for the story being told.
- Do not manufacture sophistication with extra adjectives. Words such as "rigorous,"
  "robust," "sophisticated," "complex," "production," "strategic," and "advanced" should
  appear only when the surrounding facts make the adjective informative.
- For technical applications, sound like a practitioner describing work to another practitioner.
  Preserve standard notation and concrete numbers, but do not explain obvious methodological
  virtues unless the decision itself is the point of the story.
- Do not just restate skills the role asks for (unless the posting asks for confirmation).
- Other than the first and last closing pragraph, at most 3 body paragraphs are permitted, preferably 2. 
  Long resumes are an AI use give away, and recruiters are for the most part skimming.
